use /lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Positioning:
    description:
        Positioning places a popup next to the thing it belongs to, such as a
        dropdown under its button, and keeps it on screen. It belongs to the
        component that has both the popup and the thing it's attached to.

    placement:
        The component says where it would like the popup, such as below the
        button and lined up with its left edge. This is a preference, not a
        promise. The component also decides whether the popup must be at least
        as wide as the thing it's attached to, as a Select's list of options
        usually is.

    collision:
        If the popup would run off the edge of the screen, it slides sideways
        to stay on screen. If that isn't enough, it moves to the other side,
        such as opening above instead of below. If it fits on neither side, it
        stays where it was asked to go, gets shorter, and its contents scroll.
        How it is made shorter is up to the chosen library.

    tracking:
        The popup moves with the thing it's attached to, whether the page
        scrolls, the window resizes, or the layout changes. It is never left
        behind in the wrong place. If the thing it's attached to scrolls out
        of view, the popup is hidden with it but stays open, and reappears when
        it scrolls back.

    reporting:
        The component is told where the popup actually ended up, such as above
        instead of below. That lets it point an arrow the right way, or animate
        the popup in from the right edge.

    nonGoals:
        - The popup is never moved next to something else. When nothing fits,
            it stays with the thing it was attached to
